            <p>The blue color of the sky comes from a phenomenon called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>. Sunlight contains all the colors of the rainbow, each with different wavelengths. Blue light has shorter, smaller waves compared to red light's longer waves. When sunlight passes through Earth's atmosphere, it collides with gas molecules (mostly nitrogen and oxygen). Because blue light waves are smaller, they scatter in all directions much more easily than red light does. When we look up, we see this scattered blue light coming from everywhere in the sky. That's why the sky is blue during the day—especially when the sun is high.</p>
            <p>Interestingly, this same scattering makes sunsets red! When the sun is low on the horizon, sunlight has to travel through more atmosphere, and most of the blue light gets scattered away before reaching our eyes, leaving the longer-wavelength red and orange colors to dominate.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="section" id="phd">
            <h2>For a Physics PhD</h2>
            <p>The sky's blue color is explained by <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>, named after Lord Rayleigh who first described it in the late 19th century. The scattering intensity <em>I</em> of light by particles much smaller than the wavelength of light is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the wavelength:</p>
            <p>$$I \propto \frac{1}{\lambda^4}$$</p>
            <p>This strong wavelength dependence means that shorter wavelengths (blue ~450 nm) scatter <strong>~10 times more</strong> than longer wavelengths (red ~650 nm).</p>
            <p>Key physical parameters:</p>
