                <li><strong>Solar spectrum:</strong> The Sun emits roughly white light with significant intensity across the visible spectrum</li>
                <li><strong>Atmospheric composition:</strong> Primarily nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%) with appropriate number density at sea level (~2.5 × 10<sup>19</sup> molecules/cm³)</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Why not other colors?</strong> While violet light scatters even more than blue (violet ~400 nm), two factors prevent us from seeing a violet sky:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>The Sun's emission spectrum has less intensity at violet wavelengths</li>
                <li>Human eye sensitivity peaks in the green-yellow range and has reduced sensitivity at violet wavelengths</li>
            </ol>
            <p><strong>Sunset phenomenon:</strong> At low solar angles, the path length through atmosphere increases dramatically (up to 38 times at 5° elevation). The extensive scattering removes most blue and green wavelengths, allowing longer wavelengths (reds ~650-700 nm) to dominate the transmitted light. Multiple scattering events and aerosol contributions further modify the observed colors.</p>
            <p><strong>Bonus:</strong> The sky would be black (space-like) if Earth had no atmosphere, since without scattering there would be no blue light reaching our eyes from all directions.</p>
